From c3eefe3832276228b4ed7aa168bd24fd20b1fba1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 00:22:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(moe): deterministic fused decode keeps one task per
 slice

Deterministic output made the fused dynamic kernel collapse every N128
intermediate slice of an M tile into one task and accumulate the slices into
the route row by read-modify-write. The atomic path runs one task per slice
and adds each FC2 partial atomically, so at decode (tens of routed rows, a
few M tiles) the collapse left most of the resident grid idle: DeepSeek V4.1
Flash decode fell 9-17% with deterministic output.

For W4A8 SiLU launches without a materialized intermediate, keep one task
per slice and store each slice's FC2 partial in its own route-output row,
pair * slices + slice, with one vector store. The fixed-order top-k sum then
reduces num_topk * slices rows per token. Every valid location has one
producer, so phase 0 only zeros the rows of pairs routed to no local expert.
One helper resolves the slice count for planning (route-output capacity),
the kernel specialization and the top-k sum; launches whose partial rows
would exceed 8192, and materialized launches (split, M=1, fused phase B),
keep one row per pair. The flag joins the compile key only when set, so
atomic and existing deterministic specializations keep their keys.
